## Background

Rheo has a reference-based integration test suite in `crates/tests/` that snapshot-compares HTML/PDF/EPUB outputs. There is currently no mechanism to verify that real-world Rheo projects continue to compile as the codebase evolves. This issue adds the plumbing for a new 'compatibility test' layer.

## Goal

Create infrastructure that can clone a public GitHub repo, patch its rheo.toml version field, run `rheo compile` against it, and assert the exit code is 0. All compat tests must be gated behind a `RUN_COMPAT_TESTS=1` environment variable (consistent with the existing `RUN_HTML_TESTS=1`, `RUN_PDF_TESTS=1`, `RUN_EPUB_TESTS=1` gates).

## Files to create/modify

### 1. `crates/tests/src/helpers/remote.rs` (NEW)

Implement three public functions:

```rust
/// Clone a public GitHub repo using `git clone --depth 1`.
/// Destination: `crates/tests/store/compat/<name>/`.
/// If the destination already exists, skip cloning (fast local re-runs).
/// Returns the path to the cloned directory.
pub fn clone_repo(url: &str, name: &str) -> PathBuf

/// Patch the `version` field in `<project>/rheo.toml` to match
/// env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"). Overrides whatever version the external
/// project declares, so version-mismatch errors don't mask real failures.
/// Does nothing if no rheo.toml is present.
pub fn patch_rheo_version(project_path: &Path)

/// Clone the repo, patch its version, run `rheo compile <project_path>`,
/// and panic with full stdout+stderr if exit code is non-zero.
pub fn run_compat(url: &str, name: &str)
```

Implementation notes:
- `clone_repo`: use `std::process::Command` to run `git clone --depth 1 <url> <dest>`. Compute dest as `PathBuf::from(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR")).join("store/compat").join(name)`. If dest already exists, return it immediately.
- `patch_rheo_version`: read rheo.toml with `std::fs::read_to_string`, replace the `version = "..."` line using a regex or simple string replacement, write back with `std::fs::write`. Reference: the version-injection logic in `crates/tests/src/helpers/test_store.rs` — but this must *override* an existing value, not just inject a missing one.
- `run_compat`: calls clone_repo, then patch_rheo_version, then builds the rheo binary path using `env!("CARGO_BIN_EXE_rheo")` (same mechanism as `crates/tests/tests/harness.rs`). Runs `rheo compile <cloned_path>`. Sets `TYPST_IGNORE_SYSTEM_FONTS=1` on the command. On non-zero exit, panics with a message containing full stdout and stderr.

### 2. `crates/tests/src/helpers/mod.rs` (MODIFY)

Add `pub mod remote;` alongside existing module declarations.

### 3. `crates/tests/tests/compat.rs` (NEW)

Create the test binary skeleton including the `smoke_tests!` macro definition, ready for repo entries to be added (by rheo-3cr):

```rust
use rheo_tests::helpers::remote::run_compat;

fn compat_enabled() -> bool {
    std::env::var("RUN_COMPAT_TESTS").as_deref() == Ok("1")
}

macro_rules! smoke_tests {
    ( $( ($name:ident, $url:expr) ),* $(,)? ) => {
        $(
            ::paste::paste! {
                #[test]
                fn [<smoke_ $name>]() {
                    if !compat_enabled() { return; }
                    run_compat($url, stringify!($name));
                }
            }
        )*
    };
}

// Repos are registered in rheo-3cr
smoke_tests! {}
```

The macro takes `(name, url)` entries — two fields only. The function name `smoke_<name>` is generated automatically. The `name` identifier is the repo slug (last URL path segment with `.` replaced by `_`), which is trivially derivable from the URL without a separate choice.

### 4. `crates/tests/Cargo.toml` (MODIFY)

Add the new test binary entry and the `paste` dev-dependency (needed for identifier concatenation in the macro):

```toml
[[test]]
name = "compat"
path = "tests/compat.rs"
```

```toml
[dev-dependencies]
paste = "1"
```

## Reference files

- `crates/tests/src/helpers/test_store.rs` — version injection pattern
- `crates/tests/tests/harness.rs` — RUN_* env var gating, TYPST_IGNORE_SYSTEM_FONTS, rheo binary invocation

## Acceptance criteria

- `cargo build --test compat` passes with no warnings
- `cargo test --test compat` (without RUN_COMPAT_TESTS=1) completes immediately with 0 tests run
- `run_compat` is callable from test code

The OpenHandle::Server variant uses Box<dyn Any + Send + Sync> which cannot be used by the CLI without downcasting to HtmlServerHandle, breaking the plugin abstraction entirely.

When watch mode is implemented, the watch loop needs to:
- Call the reload callback after compilation
- Keep the runtime/task alive for the server's lifetime
- Possibly get the URL for logging

None of this is possible without downcast_ref::<HtmlServerHandle>() in the CLI.

Fix: Define a ServerHandle trait in core (crates/core/src/plugins.rs:10) with the operations the engine needs:

pub trait ServerHandle: Send + Sync {
    fn url(&self) -> &str;
    fn reload(&self);
}

pub enum OpenHandle {
    Server(Box<dyn ServerHandle>),
    Direct,
    None,
}

HtmlServerHandle implements ServerHandle. The CLI only calls handle.reload() and handle.url().

Severity: High — blocks watch mode
Scope: core/html

Background: FormatPlugin::map_packages_to_assets at crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs:527-597 currently does two unrelated jobs in its default implementation: (1) parses @preview/<name>:<version> and local-path package specifiers into resolved filesystem locations, and (2) emits a synthetic PluginAssets block with copy=["**/*"] and dest=<name>. We want to split (1) out of the trait so plugins receive already-resolved typed packages and can override only the asset-shape decision in (2).

Implementation steps:

1. In crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs, add a public type near the existing PackageAssets struct (line 260):

   pub struct ResolvedPackage {
       pub name: String,        // dest name (package name or final path component)
       pub source_root: PathBuf,
   }

   Do NOT add `raw` or `origin` fields. They have no consumer in any of the chained issues (rh-B, rh-C). Keep the type minimal — match codebase style of small, purpose-built types like AssetConfig (line 44) and PackageAssets (line 260). If a future caller needs the spec or origin discriminator, add it then.

2. Add a public free function in the same file:

   pub fn resolve_packages(
       packages: &[String],
       project_root: &Path,
       cache_dir: &Path,
   ) -> Result<Vec<ResolvedPackage>>

   Move all parsing currently inside map_packages_to_assets (lines 534-584) into this function. Preserve every existing error message verbatim. There are 4 distinct messages — do not miss any:
     - "package '{}' is missing a version (expected @preview/<name>:<version>)"
     - "package '{}' has empty name or version"
     - "package '{}' not found in cache at '{}' — run a Typst compile first so the package is fetched"
     - "package '{}' uses an unsupported namespace (only @preview is supported)"

   Re-export resolve_packages and ResolvedPackage through the existing crate root path used for FormatPlugin and PackageAssets (likely rheo_core::plugins::*). The CLI rewrite in step 5 must use the actual export path.

3. Add a helper in crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs:

   pub fn default_package_assets(pkg: &ResolvedPackage) -> PackageAssets {
       PackageAssets {
           assets: PluginAssets {
               copy: vec!["**/*".to_string()],
               dest: Some(pkg.name.clone()),
               extra: toml::map::Map::new(),
           },
           source_root: pkg.source_root.clone(),
       }
   }

   This is so rh-C's HTML override can reuse the body and only add `extra` entries, instead of duplicating the construction. Eliminates drift between the default and the html override.

4. Change the FormatPlugin trait method signature in the same file to:

   fn map_packages_to_assets(&self, packages: &[ResolvedPackage]) -> Vec<PackageAssets> {
       packages.iter().map(default_package_assets).collect()
   }

   The method is now infallible (no Result) since resolution already succeeded.

5. Update the CLI call site in crates/cli/src/lib.rs:600-605. Replace:

       let package_blocks = plugin.map_packages_to_assets(
           plugin_section.packages(),
           &project.root,
           &typst_cache_dir,
       )?;

   with:

       let resolved_packages = rheo_core::plugins::resolve_packages(
           plugin_section.packages(),
           &project.root,
           &typst_cache_dir,
       )?;
       let package_blocks = plugin.map_packages_to_assets(&resolved_packages);

   Use whatever the existing import path for the plugins module is in this file.

6. Run: cargo build && cargo fmt && cargo clippy -- -D warnings && cargo test. All existing tests must still pass.

Acceptance: trait method takes &[ResolvedPackage] and returns Vec<PackageAssets>; @preview parsing lives in resolve_packages; default_package_assets helper exists and is reused by trait default; existing behavior for packages = ["./pkg", "@preview/foo:1.0.0"] is unchanged.

Background: PluginContext.assets (crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs:91) currently maps each AssetConfig name to a single Asset. With multi-source support, each name can resolve to multiple Assets (default copy-each) or one (custom combiner producing a bundle). This issue is the type-level change; resolution semantics change in a follow-up.

Steps:

1. In crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs:91, change the field type:

   pub assets: &'a HashMap<&'static str, Vec<Asset>>,

2. In crates/cli/src/lib.rs:
   - Line 334: `resolved_assets: &'a HashMap<&'static str, Vec<Asset>>` in PerFileCtx.
   - Line 382: change `resolve_assets` return type to `Result<HashMap<&'static str, Vec<Asset>>>`.
   - Lines 405-412: replace `resolved.insert(name, Asset { ... })` with `resolved.insert(name, vec![Asset { ... }])`. (Full rewrite happens in the follow-up issue; keep behavior unchanged here.)
   - Lines 360, 532, 553: `assets: &resolved_assets` already passes a reference; type inference handles the new shape.

3. In crates/html/src/lib.rs::compile (lines 101-133), update the asset lookup:

   let css_vec = ctx.assets.get(&STYLESHEETS);
   let html_string = if let Some(css_assets) = css_vec.filter(|v| !v.is_empty()) {
       for a in css_assets { info!("Found CSS stylesheet: {}", a.resolved_path.display()); }
       let css_paths: Vec<&str> = css_assets.iter().map(|a| a.built_relative_path.as_str()).collect();
       let js_paths: Vec<&str> = ctx.assets.get(&SCRIPTS)
           .map(|v| v.iter().map(|a| a.built_relative_path.as_str()).collect())
           .unwrap_or_default();
       html_utils::inject_head_links(&html_string, &[], &css_paths, &js_paths)?
   } else {
       info!("No stylesheet found, using default");
       html_utils::inject_inline_styles(&html_string, &[DEFAULT_STYLESHEET])?
   };

4. Update the five resolve_assets unit tests in crates/cli/src/lib.rs `mod tests` (lines ~1029-1184) to assert on `resolved.get("css_stylesheet").unwrap()[0].built_relative_path` etc.

Acceptance:
- cargo build && cargo test --workspace passes
- Manual smoke: cargo run -- compile <some-html-project> still produces working HTML with <link> and <script> tags pointing to the same files as before
- No new behavior; pure type-level change with vec wrapping at the seams

Depends on: rheo-6m0 (AssetCombine trait + AssetConfig.combine field must exist before code references the new shape)

Add a per-block destination override to the asset-block schema and expose an accessor that preserves which block contributed a given override. This is the foundation for the `dest` feature on `[[plugin.assets]]` blocks.

Background: A `[[plugin.assets]]` block in rheo.toml today supports `copy` (glob patterns) and AssetConfig path overrides like `js_scripts`/`css_stylesheet`. We are adding a new `dest` key that prefixes a destination subdirectory for every file produced by that block. This issue only adds the field and accessor — no behavior change yet. Issues 2 and 3 will consume the new field.

Steps:

1. In `crates/core/src/config.rs`, modify the `PluginAssets` struct (lines 34-45) to add a `dest` field:

```rust
pub struct PluginAssets {
    #[serde(default)]
    pub copy: Vec<String>,

    /// Optional destination subdirectory (relative to plugin output dir)
    /// for every file produced by this block. When set:
    /// - named asset overrides (e.g. `js_scripts`) are placed at
    ///   `<plugin_output_dir>/<dest>/<basename>` (source directory stripped);
    /// - `copy` glob matches are placed at
    ///   `<plugin_output_dir>/<dest>/<rel>` where `<rel>` is the source's
    ///   project-root-relative path (structure preserved).
    #[serde(default)]
    pub dest: Option<String>,

    #[serde(flatten, default)]
    pub extra: toml::Table,
}
```

2. Update the rustdoc above `PluginAssets` to describe the new field.

3. In the `impl PluginSection` block (lines 267-288), add a new accessor that returns block-context alongside each match:

```rust
/// Collect every (block, override-value) pair for `key` across all
/// asset blocks, in declared order. Used by callers that need each
/// block's `dest` to compute output paths.
pub fn get_strings_with_block(&self, key: &str)
    -> Vec<(&PluginAssets, &str)>
{
    self.asset_blocks()
        .iter()
        .filter_map(|b| {
            b.extra.get(key)
                .and_then(|v| v.as_str())
                .map(|s| (b, s))
        })
        .collect()
}
```

Keep `get_string` and `get_strings` unchanged for backward compatibility.

4. Update `CLAUDE.md` `## rheo.toml` section to document the `dest` key under `[[plugin.assets]]` (one short line is enough).

5. Add a unit test in the existing `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` of `config.rs` (search for `#[test]` blocks around line 621) verifying:
   - `[[html.assets]]` blocks deserialize with `dest = "allassets"`
   - `dest` defaults to `None` when omitted
   - `get_strings_with_block("js_scripts")` returns the correct `(block, value)` pairs across two blocks where one has `dest` and the other does not

Acceptance criteria:
- `cargo build` clean
- `cargo test -p rheo-core` passes including new tests
- `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` clean
- No behavior change yet: existing integration tests still pass (this issue only adds the field and accessor; the field is unused)

## Background

PluginSection now has bundle: Option<bool> (added in rheo-pn3). The CLI's perform_compilation() in crates/cli/src/lib.rs currently dispatches to compile_bundle() when plugin.uses_bundle_api() is true and the spine is not merged. It needs to also check plugin_section.bundle so that bundle = false in rheo.toml routes to compile_per_file() instead.

## Depends on

rheo-pn3 must be completed first (the bundle field must exist on PluginSection).

## File to edit

crates/cli/src/lib.rs

## Task

In perform_compilation() (around line 622-660), find the dispatch block:

```rust
if \!spine.merge && plugin.uses_bundle_api() {
    compile_bundle(...)
} else if spine.merge {
    compile_merged(...)
} else {
    compile_per_file(...)
}
```

Change the first condition to:

```rust
let use_bundle = \!spine.merge
    && plugin.uses_bundle_api()
    && plugin_section.bundle.unwrap_or(true);

if use_bundle {
    compile_bundle(...)
} else if spine.merge {
    compile_merged(...)
} else {
    compile_per_file(...)
}
```

No other changes needed. compile_bundle(), compile_merged(), and compile_per_file() are unchanged.

## Why unwrap_or(true)

None means 'use the plugin default', and for HTML/PDF plugins the default is true (uses_bundle_api() = true). This preserves backward compatibility — all existing projects without a bundle key continue to compile via the bundle API.

## Expected outcome

- When [html] bundle = false in rheo.toml, HTML compilation routes through compile_per_file() (calling HtmlPlugin::compile() once per .typ file with a single-file world)
- When bundle is absent or true, routing is unchanged
- cargo test passes, cargo clippy -- -D warnings passes

## Background

This is part of a feature to auto-detect Rheo packages from Typst import statements. After resolving a package import to a local directory (see companion issue `rheo-9dl`), this issue reads the package's typst.toml manifest and converts any [tool.rheo.{format}] section into a `PackageAssets` value that flows into the existing `resolve_assets()` pipeline.

The [tool.rheo.html] section in a package's typst.toml looks like:
```toml
[tool.rheo.html]
js_scripts = "dist/lib.js"
css_stylesheet = "index.css"
```

Field names must exactly match the asset config keys the plugin expects (`css_stylesheet` singular, `js_scripts` plural — see `crates/html/src/lib.rs:41-42`). Paths are relative to the package's `source_root` and will be resolved against it by the existing `resolve_assets()` machinery.

## Design notes — addressed in this issue

- `dest` is set to `"{namespace}/{name}"` (e.g. `"rheo/slides"`). This diverges from `default_package_assets` (`crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs:340-348`), which uses just `pkg.name`. The reason: auto-detected packages from arbitrary namespaces can collide (`@a/foo` vs `@b/foo`), so qualifying with the namespace prevents output-path collisions. The explicit-declaration path keeps short `dest = name` because users typically curate that list.
- IO and TOML parse errors are surfaced via `tracing::warn!` rather than silently dropped. This matches the codebase's "best effort with diagnostic" pattern (e.g. `crates/cli/src/lib.rs:564` for missing asset overrides). A malformed `typst.toml` should not silently disable auto-detection.
- The function takes `search_dirs` so tests don't depend on `dirs::data_dir()` / `dirs::cache_dir()`. Production wrapper supplies system dirs.
- No `already_declared` parameter for now: raw spec strings from `plugin_section.packages()` are heterogeneous (relative paths, `@preview/...:v`) and won't match auto-detected `@<ns>/<name>:<v>` strings. Adding a dedupe filter is dead code until `resolve_packages` is generalized to accept non-`@preview` namespaces (tracked separately).

## Relevant existing code

- `crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs:259-264` — `PackageAssets { assets: PluginAssets, source_root: PathBuf }`.
- `crates/core/src/config.rs:36-56` — `PluginAssets { copy: Vec<String>, dest: Option<String>, extra: toml::Table }`.
- `crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs:340-348` — `default_package_assets` for comparison.
- `crates/cli/src/lib.rs:459-621` — `resolve_assets()` reads `package.assets.extra` and resolves paths against `package.source_root`.
- `crates/html/src/lib.rs:41-101` — HTML plugin asset config names (`css_stylesheet`, `js_scripts`).
- `crates/core/src/plugins/typst_manifest.rs` — module created by `rheo-9dl` (extends `ResolvedPackage` with `namespace`/`version`).

## Steps to implement

1. In `crates/core/src/plugins/typst_manifest.rs`, add:

```rust
use crate::config::PluginAssets;
use crate::plugins::{PackageAssets, ResolvedPackage};
use tracing::warn;

/// Reads `{pkg.source_root}/typst.toml` and returns a `PackageAssets` for
/// `format_name` if `[tool.rheo.{format_name}]` exists and is non-empty.
/// Returns `None` otherwise. IO and parse errors are logged via warn!.
pub fn manifest_package_assets(pkg: &ResolvedPackage, format_name: &str) -> Option<PackageAssets> {
    let manifest_path = pkg.source_root.join("typst.toml");
    if !manifest_path.is_file() {
        return None;
    }
    let content = match std::fs::read_to_string(&manifest_path) {
        Ok(c) => c,
        Err(e) => {
            warn!(path = %manifest_path.display(), error = %e, "could not read typst.toml for auto-detect");
            return None;
        }
    };
    let toml: toml::Value = match toml::from_str(&content) {
        Ok(t) => t,
        Err(e) => {
            warn!(path = %manifest_path.display(), error = %e, "could not parse typst.toml for auto-detect");
            return None;
        }
    };
    let section = toml.get("tool")?.get("rheo")?.get(format_name)?.as_table()?;
    if section.is_empty() {
        return None;
    }
    let extra: toml::map::Map<String, toml::Value> = section.clone().into_iter().collect();
    let namespace = pkg.namespace.as_deref().unwrap_or("");
    let dest = if namespace.is_empty() {
        pkg.name.clone()
    } else {
        format!("{}/{}", namespace, pkg.name)
    };
    Some(PackageAssets {
        assets: PluginAssets {
            copy: vec![],
            dest: Some(dest),
            extra,
        },
        source_root: pkg.source_root.clone(),
    })
}

/// Scans `import_paths`, locates each package via `find_package_in_dirs`,
/// reads its manifest, and returns `PackageAssets` blocks for `format_name`.
/// Silently skips packages not present locally or with no matching section.
pub fn detect_manifest_package_assets_in_dirs(
    import_paths: &[String],
    format_name: &str,
    search_dirs: &[PathBuf],
) -> Vec<PackageAssets> {
    import_paths
        .iter()
        .filter_map(|p| find_package_in_dirs(p, search_dirs))
        .filter_map(|pkg| manifest_package_assets(&pkg, format_name))
        .collect()
}

/// Production wrapper using Typst's system data/cache dirs.
pub fn detect_manifest_package_assets(import_paths: &[String], format_name: &str) -> Vec<PackageAssets> {
    let dirs: Vec<PathBuf> = [
        dirs::data_dir().map(|d| d.join("typst/packages")),
        dirs::cache_dir().map(|d| d.join("typst/packages")),
    ]
    .into_iter()
    .flatten()
    .collect();
    detect_manifest_package_assets_in_dirs(import_paths, format_name, &dirs)
}
```

