## Background

When compiling a project file-by-file (e.g., all HTML files in a multi-chapter project), `crates/cli/src/lib.rs` reuses a single `RheoWorld` across files (~line 554-559):

```rust
for typ_file in &files {
    existing_world.set_main(typ_file)?;
    existing_world.reset();  // clears entire slots HashMap
    compile_one_file(existing_world, ...)?;
}
```

`RheoWorld::reset()` (`crates/core/src/world.rs:108-111`) clears the entire `slots: Mutex<HashMap<FileId, FileSlot>>` cache. This means any shared imports (e.g. `utils.typ` imported by every chapter) are re-read from disk and re-transformed on every compilation. For a 50-chapter project, `utils.typ` is read and transformed 50 times.

**Why non-main slots are safe to preserve:**
`RheoWorld::source()` (`world.rs:274-317`) injects content differently based on whether the requested file is the main file (`id == self.main`, lines 293-300). Non-main files only get the `target()` polyfill injected — a constant that depends only on `format_name`, which never changes between per-file compilations. Their link transformations are also deterministic given the same `format_name` and `project_root`. So cached non-main slots remain valid when the main file changes.

**Only two slots become invalid when main changes:**
1. The old main file's slot — it was cached with rheo.typ injection, now invalid if it becomes an import
2. The new main file's slot — it may have been cached as an import (polyfill-only), now needs rheo.typ injection

## Files

- **`crates/core/src/world.rs`** — `set_main()` lines 113-123, `reset()` lines 108-111
- **`crates/cli/src/lib.rs`** — per-file compilation loop (~lines 554-559, search for `set_main` + `reset`)

## Steps

1. In `world.rs`, update `set_main()` to invalidate only the affected slots:
   ```rust
   pub fn set_main(&mut self, main_file: &Path) -> Result<()> {
       let old_main = self.main;
       let main_path = crate::path_utils::canonicalize_path(main_file)?;
       let main_vpath = VirtualPath::within_root(&main_path, &self.root).ok_or_else(|| {
           RheoError::path(&main_path, "main file must be within root directory")
       })?;
       self.main = FileId::new(None, main_vpath);

       // Invalidate only the two slots whose content depends on which file is main.
       // All other slots (imports, packages) are deterministic given format_name + root.
       let mut slots = self.slots.lock();
       slots.remove(&old_main);
       slots.remove(&self.main);

       Ok(())
   }
   ```

2. In `lib.rs`, remove the `existing_world.reset()` call that follows `set_main()` in the per-file compilation loop. (`reset()` is still needed in watch mode where disk content can change between compilations — do not remove those call sites.)

3. Verify that `reset()` call sites in watch mode are untouched. (Search for `reset()` in lib.rs; there should be a separate call for the watch loop — keep that one.)

4. Run `cargo test` — all tests must pass.
5. Compile a multi-file project (`cargo run -- compile <path> --html`) and confirm it produces correct output for all files.
6. Run `cargo fmt && cargo clippy -- -D warnings`.

## Expected outcome

Shared imports are read from disk and transformed once per format compilation session rather than once per main-file compilation. No behaviour change in output — slots for non-main files contain exactly the same content they would have produced if re-computed.

